7
00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:20.524
Ch 02, Ch 26, Ch 31, Ch 34, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.804 --> 00:00:35.097
Cost as a first-class architecture quality: turning a cloud bill into unit economics, rightsizing and autoscaling, commitment strategy under uncertainty, and the storage and data-transfer decisions nobody makes on purpose.

9
00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:43.719
Four runnable labs; every price in this chapter is an illustrative assumption, not a current vendor rate, substitute your contract's numbers.

10
00:00:45.069 --> 00:00:47.309
L1: unit economics from a bill.

11
00:00:47.589 --> 00:01:10.522
A 2.28 crore rupees per month bill (illustrative) became 0.76 rupeesper order; driver-based allocation moved up to 3.7 lakh rupees per month between teams compared with an even split; 4.5% of spend was untagged; the most expensive-looking service returned 8.5x its cost in measured uplift.

12
00:01:10.802 --> 00:01:13.127
L2: rightsizing and autoscaling.

13
00:01:13.407 --> 00:01:33.994
Over 14 days with a 5.3x festival spike, a static fleet sized for peak ran at 4% of needed capacity; reactive autoscaling cost a fraction but was overloaded for the first 5 minutes of the sale; adding a scheduled pre-scale reached 0 overloaded minutes at 7% of the static fleet's cost.

14
00:01:35.344 --> 00:01:37.008
L3: how much to commit.

15
00:01:37.288 --> 00:01:48.147
Against expected demand the optimum commitment is where the marginal pod is busy ≥ 60% of hours (37 pods, minus 23%).

16
00:01:48.427 --> 00:02:04.299
Under uncertain demand, committing to the p90 was worst (35.7 lakh rupees p95 regret); the curve was flat from 0.85x to 1.2x of the optimum, and growth-skew favored the upper end.

17
00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:06.541
L4: storage and transfer.

18
00:02:06.821 --> 00:02:15.803
Measured on 50,000 real-shaped events: gzip 6.3x, binary + gzip 9.0x smaller than JSON.

19
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:30.184
Three-year retention: order events are a rounding error at any policy, but logs + clickstream cost 23.2 crore rupees kept hot and raw versus 29.3 lakh rupees compressed and tiered.

20
00:02:30.464 --> 00:02:31.445
Reading time:

21
00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:33.517
110 min + code.

22
00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:35.721
Part 1.

23
00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:37.921
Introduction and Why It Matters.

24
00:02:39.171 --> 00:02:42.584
A story: the bill that doubled while traffic grew 20%.

25
00:02:42.864 --> 00:02:54.469
In the year after the microservices migration and the first festival season, the grocery platform's cloud bill went from 1.1 crore rupees to 2.3 crore rupees a month.

26
00:02:54.749 --> 00:02:56.328
Traffic grew 20%.

27
00:02:56.608 --> 00:03:04.736
The CFO asked engineering a reasonable question, what did we get for the extra 1.2 crore rupees? and nobody could answer.

28
00:03:05.016 --> 00:03:15.747
The investigation took three weeks, because the bill was organized by cloud product (EC2, RDS, data transfer) rather than by anything the business understood.

29
00:03:16.027 --> 00:03:16.944
What it found:

30
00:03:18.294 --> 00:03:20.086
Nobody owned most of the spend.

31
00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:34.382
Shared clusters, observability and networking were a third of the bill and belonged to no team; 5% was attached to resources with no tags at all, including instances from a load test that had finished four months earlier.

32
00:03:34.662 --> 00:03:36.987
The fleet was sized for Diwali, all year.

33
00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:44.499
After a festival-season outage, the team had raised every service's replica count to survive peak and never lowered it.

34
00:03:44.779 --> 00:03:47.915
Average utilisation was in single digits (L2).

35
00:03:49.265 --> 00:03:50.887
Commitments were bought at the peak.

36
00:03:51.167 --> 00:04:01.983
In a burst of cost-consciousness, someone had bought one-year reserved capacity equal to the festival fleet, which then sat idle eleven months a year, already paid for (L3).

37
00:04:02.263 --> 00:04:04.823
Logs were kept forever in the hot search cluster.

38
00:04:05.103 --> 00:04:14.063
Debug-level logs from every service, uncompressed, retained indefinitely in case we need them, the fastest-growing line on the bill (L4).

39
00:04:14.343 --> 00:04:16.689
Nobody could say whether any spend was worth it.

40
00:04:16.969 --> 00:04:24.820
The recommendations service was the obvious cut candidate by size, until someone looked at its A or B test results (L1).

41
00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.325
None of these were technology failures.

42
00:04:28.605 --> 00:04:34.471
They were decisions nobody knew they were making: defaults, one-way ratchets, and costs with no owner.

43
00:04:34.751 --> 00:04:47.253
The fix wasn't a cost-cutting sprint; it was making cost visible per team and per order, giving it owners, and treating it as an architecture quality with the same rigor as latency and availability.

44
00:04:48.603 --> 00:04:49.285
Quote:

45
00:04:49.565 --> 00:04:51.229
The thesis of this chapter.

46
00:04:51.509 --> 00:05:01.472
Cost is a quality attribute like latency or availability: it has a budget, it's shaped by architecture decisions, and it erodes unless someone owns it.

47
00:05:01.752 --> 00:05:18.563
FinOps makes it manageable by (1) allocating every rupee to an owner and a unit of business value, cost per order, not the EC2 bill; (2) optimising the few big levers, capacity matched to demand, commitments sized to the floor you're confident of,

48
00:05:18.843 --> 00:05:29.488
data kept in the cheapest tier that meets its access pattern; and (3) operating continuously, budgets, anomaly alerts, and engineers who see the cost of what they build.

49
00:05:29.768 --> 00:05:36.872
The goal is not the lowest bill; it's the most value per rupee, with effort proportionate to where the money actually goes.

50
00:05:38.222 --> 00:05:40.526
