94
00:07:32.483 --> 00:07:38.435
Run FinOps as a practice: ownership, budgets, anomalies, and cost in every design review.

95
00:07:39.785 --> 00:07:40.659
Part 2.

96
00:07:40.939 --> 00:07:42.838
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

97
00:07:44.088 --> 00:07:46.883
Intuition: cost is an architecture quality.

98
00:07:47.163 --> 00:07:52.048
In a data centre, cost was mostly a procurement decision made once every few years.

99
00:07:52.328 --> 00:08:03.613
In the cloud, every engineer spends money every day: a replica count, a log level, an instance type, a retention setting, a cross-region call pattern, each is a recurring charge.

100
00:08:03.893 --> 00:08:07.541
That makes cost an emergent property of design, like latency.

101
00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:10.083
Three consequences shape this chapter:

102
00:08:10.363 --> 00:08:13.968
Cost needs owners, just as services need on-call owners.

103
00:08:14.248 --> 00:08:17.427
Spend nobody owns grows (L1's untagged line).

104
00:08:18.777 --> 00:08:22.147
Cost must be measured in business units to be decided on.

105
00:08:22.427 --> 00:08:32.795
2.3 crore rupees invites panic; 0.76 rupeesper order, 0.12% of order value invites proportionate action.

106
00:08:33.075 --> 00:08:35.273
Cost trades off against other qualities.

107
00:08:35.553 --> 00:08:46.667
Less headroom saves money and moves you toward the queueing cliff (Ch 31 L3); cells cost more infrastructure and buy blast-radius containment (Ch 34).

108
00:08:46.947 --> 00:08:49.870
The goal is value per rupee, not minimum rupees.

109
00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:52.692
What drives a cloud bill.

110
00:08:52.972 --> 00:08:56.961
For most backend platforms, spend concentrates in a few categories:

111
00:08:58.311 --> 00:08:59.677
Here is a quick table:

112
00:08:59.957 --> 00:09:02.495
Driver, and Typical share, and Main levers.

113
00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:05.271
Compute (VMs, containers, functions).

114
00:09:05.551 --> 00:09:06.597
Typical share:

115
00:09:06.877 --> 00:09:08.391
40 to 60%.

116
00:09:08.671 --> 00:09:09.695
Main levers:

117
00:09:09.975 --> 00:09:15.138
Rightsizing, autoscaling, commitments, spot for interruptible work, efficient code.

118
00:09:15.418 --> 00:09:17.594
Databases and caches (managed).

119
00:09:17.874 --> 00:09:18.919
Typical share:

120
00:09:19.199 --> 00:09:20.799
15 to 30%.

121
00:09:21.079 --> 00:09:22.103
Main levers:

122
00:09:22.383 --> 00:09:27.717
Instance sizing, read replicas only where needed, storage tier, reserved capacity.

123
00:09:27.997 --> 00:09:30.535
Storage (block, object, search clusters).

124
00:09:30.815 --> 00:09:31.861
Typical share:

125
00:09:32.141 --> 00:09:33.613
5 to 20%.

126
00:09:33.893 --> 00:09:34.917
Main levers:

127
00:09:35.197 --> 00:09:39.442
Compression, lifecycle tiering, retention policy, deleting orphans.

128
00:09:39.722 --> 00:09:42.879
Data transfer (egress, cross-zone, cross-region).

129
00:09:43.159 --> 00:09:44.205
Typical share:

130
00:09:44.485 --> 00:09:45.829
2 to 15%.

131
00:09:46.109 --> 00:09:47.133
Main levers:

132
00:09:47.413 --> 00:09:51.935
Zone-aware routing, CDN, compression, avoiding cross-region chatter.

133
00:09:52.215 --> 00:09:54.839
Observability (logs, metrics, traces).

134
00:09:55.119 --> 00:09:56.165
Typical share:

135
00:09:56.445 --> 00:09:57.938
5 to 15%.

136
00:09:58.218 --> 00:09:59.242
Main levers:

137
00:09:59.522 --> 00:10:02.999
Log levels, sampling, retention, cardinality control.

138
00:10:04.349 --> 00:10:05.949
Non-production environments.

139
00:10:06.229 --> 00:10:07.275
Typical share:

140
00:10:07.555 --> 00:10:09.197
10 to 25%.

141
00:10:09.477 --> 00:10:10.501
Main levers:

142
00:10:10.781 --> 00:10:14.387
Shut down off-hours, smaller shapes, ephemeral environments.

143
00:10:15.737 --> 00:10:35.555
Two practical observations: non-production is often a surprisingly large slice (L1: dev or staging was 11.5 lakh rupees per month, more than several production services), and observability grows faster than traffic because every new service and feature adds logs, metrics and cardinality.

144
00:10:36.905 --> 00:10:39.359
Cost allocation and unit economics.

145
00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:42.156
Allocation assigns every rupee to an owner:

146
00:10:42.436 --> 00:10:48.559
Direct costs, resources tagged to one service or team (a service's node pool, its database).

147
00:10:48.839 --> 00:10:54.087
Shared costs, clusters, networking, observability, data platform, CI, non-prod.

148
00:10:54.367 --> 00:10:56.393
Allocate them by usage drivers:

149
00:10:56.673 --> 00:11:07.745
Kafka by bytes produced, observability by log or metric volume, NAT or egress by gigabytes out, the cluster control plane by requests or resource requests.

150
00:11:08.025 --> 00:11:12.569
Untagged costs, a governance failure to be driven to zero, not allocated.

151
00:11:13.919 --> 00:11:22.474
L1 compares an even split with driver-based allocation: the method moved up to 3.7 lakh rupees per month between teams.

152
00:11:22.754 --> 00:11:31.287
An even split is simple but distorts incentives, a low-traffic team pays for another team's logs and has no reason to reduce its own.

153
00:11:31.567 --> 00:11:35.301
Unit economics divides allocated cost by a business unit:

154
00:11:35.581 --> 00:11:37.095
Cost per order (L1:

155
00:11:37.375 --> 00:11:42.069
0.76 rupees), the headline metric for a commerce platform.

156
00:11:42.349 --> 00:11:48.578
Cost per active customer, per dark store, per tenant (Ch 30), depending on how the business grows.

157
00:11:48.858 --> 00:11:53.125
Cost per feature or service per order, which enables value questions.

158
00:11:54.475 --> 00:12:06.997
Unit cost is what makes growth legible: the bill doubling while cost per order stays flat is healthy; cost per order rising 60% (§1.1) is a problem regardless of the bill.

159
00:12:07.277 --> 00:12:13.955
Showback versus chargeback: showback reports each team's cost to them; chargeback actually bills their budget.

160
00:12:14.235 --> 00:12:22.299
Start with showback, it changes behavior once people can see their numbers, and move to chargeback only where budgets are genuinely owned.

161
00:12:23.649 --> 00:12:25.078
Value, not just cost.

162
00:12:25.358 --> 00:12:28.451
Cost numbers without value numbers lead to bad cuts.

163
00:12:28.731 --> 00:12:43.963
L1's recommendations service was among the two largest lines, and its A or B-measured uplift (illustrative: +1.2% GMV at 18% contribution margin) returned about 8.5x its cost.

164
00:12:44.243 --> 00:12:50.345
Cutting it would have saved 49 lakh rupees a month and lost 4.2 crore rupees of profit.

165
00:12:50.625 --> 00:12:52.395
Ways to connect cost to value:

166
00:12:52.675 --> 00:13:00.014
Experiments, A or B tests with and without a feature give incremental revenue; divide by the feature's allocated cost.

167
00:13:01.364 --> 00:13:06.185
Unit margin, cloud cost per order relative to contribution margin per order.

168
00:13:06.465 --> 00:13:07.297
L1:

169
00:13:07.577 --> 00:13:25.689
0.76 rupeesagainst a 650 rupeesorder, for quick commerce, rider and dark-store costs dwarf cloud cost, so cloud optimisation effort should be proportionate (remove waste and bad incentives; don't micro-optimise at the expense of delivery speed).

170
00:13:25.969 --> 00:13:33.223
Cost of downtime, the value side of reliability spend (Ch 34 cells, Ch 27 DR).

171
00:13:34.573 --> 00:13:36.365
Rightsizing and autoscaling.

172
00:13:36.645 --> 00:13:39.802
Rightsizing matches provisioned capacity to demand.

173
00:13:40.082 --> 00:13:41.575
Two decisions dominate:

174
00:13:41.855 --> 00:13:43.242
Target utilisation.

175
00:13:43.522 --> 00:14:04.130
Higher utilisation is cheaper but moves you up the queueing curve: latency rises non-linearly as utilisation approaches 100% (Ch 31 L3). ~50 to 70% CPU for latency-sensitive services is a common compromise; batch and async consumers can run hotter.

176
00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:07.759
How to cover demand the control loop can't react to in time.

177
00:14:08.039 --> 00:14:15.890
Autoscalers act on observed metrics with a lag (metric window, scale-up decision, pod scheduling, JVM warm-up).

178
00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:20.927
A demand step faster than that lag overloads you before new capacity arrives.

179
00:14:22.277 --> 00:14:29.957
L2 shows the whole trade-off on 14 days of demand with a festival spike of 5.3x a normal peak:

180
00:14:31.307 --> 00:14:32.673
Here is a quick table:

181
00:14:32.953 --> 00:14:35.193
Policy, and Cost, and Overloaded.

182
00:14:35.473 --> 00:14:37.201
Static for the all-time peak.

183
00:14:37.481 --> 00:14:38.206
Cost:

184
00:14:38.486 --> 00:14:41.622
100% (4% needed utilisation).

185
00:14:41.902 --> 00:14:42.841
Overloaded:

186
00:14:43.121 --> 00:14:44.102
0 min.

187
00:14:44.382 --> 00:14:45.961
Static for a normal day.

188
00:14:46.241 --> 00:14:48.011
Cost: ~20%.

189
00:14:48.291 --> 00:14:49.230
Overloaded:

190
00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:51.729
120 min, during the sale.

191
00:14:52.009 --> 00:14:54.846
Reactive autoscaling (60% target).

192
00:14:55.126 --> 00:14:56.939
Cost: ~7%.

193
00:14:57.219 --> 00:14:58.158
Overloaded:

194
00:14:58.438 --> 00:15:00.721
5 min, the first bucket of the sale.

195
00:15:01.001 --> 00:15:03.283
Autoscaling + scheduled pre-scale.

196
00:15:03.563 --> 00:15:05.377
Cost: ~7%.

197
00:15:05.657 --> 00:15:06.595
Overloaded:

198
00:15:06.875 --> 00:15:07.857
0 min.

199
00:15:09.207 --> 00:15:28.193
The general pattern: autoscale for the daily curve; pre-scale for known events (sales, campaigns, festival days, they're on the calendar); keep a warm pool or fast-starting images for unknown spikes; and use load shedding (Ch 18) as the last line of defense rather than paying for peak all year.

200
00:15:28.473 --> 00:15:38.692
Also rightsize the shape: memory-heavy JVM services on compute-optimised instances (or the reverse) waste money even at correct replica counts.

201
00:15:38.972 --> 00:15:48.295
Profile requests versus actual usage; Kubernetes resource requests set far above real usage are one of the most common sources of waste.

202
00:15:49.645 --> 00:15:51.437
Pricing models and commitments.

203
00:15:51.717 --> 00:15:54.405
Cloud capacity is sold in three broad ways:

204
00:15:54.685 --> 00:15:57.735
On-demand, pay per hour or second, no commitment.

205
00:15:58.015 --> 00:16:00.191
The most flexible and most expensive.

206
00:16:00.471 --> 00:16:11.927
Commitments (reserved instances, savings plans, committed-use discounts), commit to a spend or capacity level for 1 to 3 years for a discount (often 30 to 60%).

207
00:16:12.207 --> 00:16:13.701
Paid whether used or not.

208
00:16:13.981 --> 00:16:20.338
Spot or preemptible, spare capacity at a deep discount that the provider can reclaim with short notice.

209
00:16:20.618 --> 00:16:22.325
Only for interruptible work.

210
00:16:23.675 --> 00:16:35.899
The commitment rule (L3): a marginal committed unit pays off if it would be busy in at least commit price ÷ on-demand price of the hours, 60% at a 40% discount.

211
00:16:36.179 --> 00:16:41.533
So commit to the floor, not the peak: the level your fleet stays at or above most of the time.

212
00:16:41.813 --> 00:16:55.893
Against the expected demand curve, L3's optimum was 37 pods (busy 62% of hours), saving 23%; committing to the p90 (92 pods) was worse than no commitment at all.

213
00:16:56.173 --> 00:16:58.669
Under uncertainty about next year's demand:

214
00:16:58.949 --> 00:17:10.384
The cost curve is flat near the optimum (0.85x, 1.2x within ~2% expected cost), so precision matters less than avoiding the tails.

215
00:17:11.734 --> 00:17:13.569
The right hedge follows the skew.

216
00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:23.107
L3's demand was more likely to grow than shrink, so a slightly higher commitment did as well on average and better in the p95 regret.

217
00:17:23.387 --> 00:17:26.097
If the risk were a shrinking market, you'd hedge lower.

218
00:17:26.377 --> 00:17:34.547
Ratchet: commitments are easy to add and hard to exit, so commit to the confident floor and add quarterly as growth materialises.

219
00:17:34.827 --> 00:17:46.347
Spot capacity fits stateless, interruptible, retry-safe work: batch jobs, image processing, async consumers with checkpoints, CI runners, ML training with checkpointing.

220
00:17:46.627 --> 00:17:49.123
Never the checkout path or stateful primaries.

221
00:17:50.473 --> 00:17:53.225
Storage, data lifecycle and data transfer.

222
00:17:53.505 --> 00:18:04.791
Storage and transfer costs are mostly defaults nobody chose: default log levels, default retention (forever), default storage class, default load balancing across zones.

223
00:18:05.071 --> 00:18:09.423
Encoding, measured in L4 on 50,000 real-shaped order events:

224
00:18:09.703 --> 00:18:21.777
JSON 245 bytes or event; gzip 6.3x smaller; a schema'd binary encoding 6.3x; binary + gzip 9.0x.

225
00:18:22.057 --> 00:18:29.545
Schema'd formats (Protobuf, Avro, Ch 28) and columnar formats (Parquet) for analytics compound this.

226
00:18:30.895 --> 00:18:34.287
Placement and lifecycle usually matter more than encoding.

227
00:18:34.567 --> 00:18:41.522
Data has an access pattern that decays with age: hot for days, occasionally read for months, rarely for years.

228
00:18:41.802 --> 00:18:42.975
Match tiers to it:

229
00:18:44.325 --> 00:18:45.691
Here is a quick table:

230
00:18:45.971 --> 00:18:50.045
Tier, and Use, and Relative ₹/GB-month (illustrative).

231
00:18:50.325 --> 00:18:53.120
Hot database or search cluster (SSD).

232
00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:54.104
Use:

233
00:18:54.384 --> 00:18:56.603
Queried constantly, low latency.

234
00:18:56.883 --> 00:18:59.805
Relative ₹/GB-month (illustrative):

235
00:19:00.085 --> 00:19:01.237
8.0.

236
00:19:01.517 --> 00:19:03.245
Object storage, standard.

237
