Use:

238
00:19:04.509 --> 00:19:06.280
Frequent reads, analytics.

239
00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:09.483
Relative ₹/GB-month (illustrative):

240
00:19:09.763 --> 00:19:10.808
1.9.

241
00:19:11.088 --> 00:19:13.413
Object storage, infrequent access.

242
00:19:13.693 --> 00:19:14.397
Use:

243
00:19:14.677 --> 00:19:15.765
Monthly reads.

244
00:19:16.045 --> 00:19:18.968
Relative ₹/GB-month (illustrative):

245
00:19:19.248 --> 00:19:20.357
1.0.

246
00:19:20.637 --> 00:19:21.491
Archive.

247
00:19:21.771 --> 00:19:22.475
Use:

248
00:19:22.755 --> 00:19:26.829
Compliance retention, rare reads (retrieval fee, hours of delay).

249
00:19:27.109 --> 00:19:30.032
Relative ₹/GB-month (illustrative):

250
00:19:30.312 --> 00:19:31.805
0.08.

251
00:19:33.155 --> 00:19:53.699
L4's three-year retention for three streams: order events cost almost nothing at any policy (2 lakh rupees kept hot and raw); application logs and clickstream, 1,000x and 200x larger, cost 23.2 crore rupees hot and raw versus 29.3 lakh rupees compressed and tiered.

252
00:19:53.979 --> 00:20:04.454
The lesson is proportionality: rank data streams by size and optimise the big ones; the data engineers worry about (orders) is rarely the data that costs money.

253
00:20:04.734 --> 00:20:15.593
Retention policy is the biggest single lever: decide per data class how long it's needed and at what access speed, then enforce it with lifecycle rules, not good intentions.

254
00:20:15.873 --> 00:20:26.646
Regulatory retention (GST invoices, payment records, Ch 36) goes to archive tiers; debug logs rarely need more than 7 to 30 days.

255
00:20:27.996 --> 00:20:34.823
Data transfer: egress to the internet, cross-region replication and cross-zone traffic are billed per GB.

256
00:20:35.103 --> 00:20:48.265
L4 shows cross-zone transfer is small at this platform's scale (tens of thousands of rupees a month) but it becomes a top line for data-heavy systems (media, ML features, analytics replication).

257
00:20:48.545 --> 00:20:57.164
Levers: zone-aware routing, follower-fetching Kafka consumers, CDNs for egress, compression, and avoiding chatty cross-region calls.

258
00:20:58.514 --> 00:20:59.794
Designing for cost.

259
00:21:00.074 --> 00:21:04.490
Architecture decisions set the cost curve long before any optimisation:

260
00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:12.813
Statelessness and fast startup make autoscaling effective (a service that takes 3 minutes to warm up forces you to over-provision).

261
00:21:13.093 --> 00:21:21.669
Caching trades memory for database capacity; read models trade storage for query compute (Ch 07, Ch 15).

262
00:21:21.949 --> 00:21:30.887
Async processing flattens peaks: work that can wait (emails, invoices, recommendations refresh) runs off-peak on cheaper capacity.

263
00:21:31.167 --> 00:21:41.855
Multi-tenancy amortises fixed costs (Ch 30); cells multiply them (Ch 34), both are cost decisions as much as isolation decisions.

264
00:21:43.205 --> 00:21:50.821
Service granularity: each microservice carries a fixed floor (replicas for HA, pipelines, observability).

265
00:21:51.101 --> 00:21:56.819
Twenty-six services at a 3-replica HA floor is 78 pods before any traffic.

266
00:21:57.099 --> 00:22:02.901
Serverless versus containers is largely a cost-curve decision (Ch 31 L4).

267
00:22:03.181 --> 00:22:14.595
Efficient code matters at scale: a 20% CPU reduction in the hottest service is a 20% cut of its compute line, profile before scaling (Ch 25).

268
00:22:14.875 --> 00:22:22.683
Put cost in the design doc (Ch 32): estimated monthly cost, cost per unit, how it scales, and the biggest lever.

269
00:22:24.033 --> 00:22:25.547
FinOps as a practice.

270
00:22:25.827 --> 00:22:30.371
The FinOps Foundation describes a loop of three phases, run continuously:

271
00:22:30.651 --> 00:22:34.854
Inform, allocation, tagging, showback, unit economics, forecasts.

272
00:22:35.134 --> 00:22:37.289
Everyone can see what they spend and why.

273
00:22:37.569 --> 00:22:45.590
Optimise, rightsizing, autoscaling, commitments, spot, storage lifecycle, waste removal (orphans, idle environments).

274
00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:58.819
Operate, budgets per team, anomaly detection, governance (tag policies enforced at provisioning, approval for large commitments), cost reviews in design and ADRs, and continuous improvement.

275
00:22:59.099 --> 00:23:00.742
Organisational ingredients:

276
00:23:02.092 --> 00:23:06.231
Engineers own the cost of what they build, with visibility, not blame.

277
00:23:06.511 --> 00:23:11.076
Put a cost panel next to latency and error rate on every service dashboard.

278
00:23:11.356 --> 00:23:19.591
A small central FinOps function runs the tooling, buys commitments (centrally, because they're pooled across teams), and coaches teams.

279
00:23:19.871 --> 00:23:33.524
Anomaly detection: alert on day-over-day or forecast deviation per team or service, a misconfigured autoscaler or a runaway log stream should page someone within hours, not appear on next month's invoice.

280
00:23:34.874 --> 00:23:47.205
Policy as code: required tags, allowed instance families, maximum retention defaults, auto-shutdown for non-prod, enforced at provisioning (Terraform policy, admission controllers).

281
00:23:48.555 --> 00:23:51.264
Proportionality: where FinOps effort pays.

282
00:23:51.544 --> 00:23:54.957
The recurring lesson of all four labs is proportionality:

283
00:23:55.237 --> 00:24:05.243
Cloud cost per order was 0.76 rupeesagainst a 650 rupeesorder (L1), meaningful, but not the business's biggest cost.

284
00:24:05.523 --> 00:24:07.955
Don't slow feature delivery to shave pennies.

285
00:24:08.235 --> 00:24:20.480
The big wins are structural and one-time: stop paying for peak all year (L2), stop committing to the peak (L3), stop keeping logs hot forever (L4), own the untagged spend (L1).

286
00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:29.059
Tiny streams stay tiny whatever you do (order events in L4); optimise the streams and services at the top of the ranked list.

287
00:24:30.409 --> 00:24:38.067
Rank, then act: sort spend by owner and driver, attack the top five lines, and put guardrails in place so they don't grow back.

288
00:24:39.417 --> 00:24:40.313
