Adding a scheduled pre-scale for the known event removes that gap for ~100 extra pod-hours.

418
00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:03.773
Known events belong on a calendar, not in the autoscaler's reaction time.

419
00:39:05.123 --> 00:39:07.491
L3, How much capacity to commit to.

420
00:39:07.771 --> 00:39:08.710
Requirement:

421
00:39:08.990 --> 00:39:13.449
Finance can get a 40% discount if we commit to a year of capacity.

422
00:39:13.729 --> 00:39:16.929
How much should we commit, given we don't know next year's demand?

423
00:39:17.209 --> 00:39:26.553
A year of hourly demand for the whole platform (daily curve, weekends, festival evenings, steady background load, 24-pod HA floor).

424
00:39:26.833 --> 00:39:47.590
Step 1 sweeps the commitment level against expected demand; step 2 runs 20,000 futures with demand scaled by a triangular(0.7, 1.0, 1.5) factor and compares expected cost, p95 cost, and p95 regret versus the hindsight-optimal commitment.

425
00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:51.671
Here is the Java code, CommitL3.java.

426
00:39:51.951 --> 00:39:56.132
It runs to 132 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

427
00:39:56.412 --> 00:40:03.281
CommitL3.java, run with: java CommitL3.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

428
00:40:03.561 --> 00:40:05.396
HOW MUCH CAPACITY TO COMMIT TO.

429
00:40:05.676 --> 00:40:19.201
Cloud providers discount capacity you commit to for a year (reserved instances or savings. plans), but you pay for it every hour whether you use it or not; anything above the commitment runs on-demand.

430
00:40:22.051 --> 00:40:22.969
On screen:

431
00:40:23.249 --> 00:40:31.249
Expected demand next year: floor 29, median 44, p90 92, peak 435 pods.

432
00:40:31.529 --> 00:40:32.553
All on-demand:

433
00:40:32.833 --> 00:40:41.345
85.2 lakh rupees per year (illustrative). commit annual cost versus on-demand hours the marginal pod is busy.

434
00:40:41.625 --> 00:40:47.086
0 pods 85.2 L +0.0% 100%.

435
00:40:47.366 --> 00:40:53.062
24 pods 70.1 L -17.8% 100%.

436
00:40:53.342 --> 00:41:00.361
37 pods ← 65.6 L -23.0% 62%.

437
00:41:00.641 --> 00:41:06.699
40 pods 65.6 L -23.0% 58%.

438
00:41:06.979 --> 00:41:12.889
60 pods 71.7 L -15.9% 28%.

439
00:41:13.169 --> 00:41:18.267
100 pods 98.3 L +15.4% 8%.

440
00:41:18.547 --> 00:41:24.819
140 pods 133.9 L +57.2% 1%.

441
00:41:25.099 --> 00:41:40.694
435 pods 411.5 L +383.1% 0%. then optimum against EXPECTED demand: commit 37 pods (busy 62% of hours, just above.

442
00:41:40.974 --> 00:41:42.489
The rest is in the chapter.

443
00:41:45.339 --> 00:41:46.192
Reading it:

444
00:41:46.472 --> 00:41:59.955
The break-even rule works exactly: the optimum (37 pods) is where the marginal pod is busy 62% of hours, just above the 60% break-even (10.80 or 18).

445
00:42:00.235 --> 00:42:03.136
It saves 23% versus all on-demand.

446
00:42:03.416 --> 00:42:13.827
Committing to the p90 is the expensive mistake: worse expected cost than everything else, and 35.7 lakh rupees of p95 regret.

447
00:42:14.107 --> 00:42:16.304
Committing to the peak would be far worse still.

448
00:42:17.654 --> 00:42:30.625
Under uncertainty the curve is flat between 0.85x and 1.2x of the optimum (expected cost within ~2%), so avoiding the tails matters more than precision.

449
00:42:30.905 --> 00:42:32.419
The hedge follows the skew.

450
00:42:32.699 --> 00:42:46.139
Demand here is more likely to grow than shrink (the triangle's mean is above its mode), so 1.2x the expected-demand optimum ties on expected cost and has the lowest p95 regret.

451
00:42:46.419 --> 00:42:49.171
For a shrinking-market risk you'd hedge the other way.

452
00:42:50.521 --> 00:42:53.956
L4, Storage and data transfer: measure, then rank.

453
00:42:54.236 --> 00:42:55.175
Requirement:

454
00:42:55.455 --> 00:42:58.953
Should we switch our event log to a binary format to save money?

455
00:42:59.233 --> 00:43:00.983
And what about cross-zone traffic?

456
00:43:01.263 --> 00:43:07.684
Part 1 measures four encodings of 50,000 real-shaped order events with java.util.zip.

457
00:43:07.964 --> 00:43:23.175
Parts 2 and 3 apply the measured compression ratio to three real data streams, order events, application logs (volume from L1's usage data) and app clickstream, and price three years of retention and monthly cross-zone transfer.

458
00:43:24.525 --> 00:43:27.490
Here is the Java code, StorageL4.java.

459
00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:31.951
It runs to 182 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

460
00:43:32.231 --> 00:43:39.527
StorageL4.java, run with: java StorageL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

461
00:43:39.807 --> 00:43:44.159
STORAGE AND DATA-TRANSFER COSTS, the lines on the bill nobody designed.

462
00:43:44.439 --> 00:43:57.773
Part 1 MEASURES real encodings of 50,000 order. events (JSON, gzip'd JSON, a compact binary encoding, gzip'd binary) with java.util.zip; Part 2 extrapolates to the.

463
00:44:00.623 --> 00:44:01.540
On screen:

464
00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:08.263
Part 1, measured encodings of 50,000 real-shaped order events (java.util.zip, level.

465
00:44:08.543 --> 00:44:11.977
6): encoding total bytes or event versus JSON.

466
00:44:12.257 --> 00:44:19.511
JSON 11,981 KB 245.4 1.0x.

467
00:44:19.791 --> 00:44:40.953
JSON + gzip 1,912 KB 39.2 6.3x. compact binary (varints) 1,900 KB 38.9 6.3x. compact binary + gzip 1,332 KB 27.3 9.0x.

468
00:44:41.233 --> 00:44:43.985
Part 2, three data streams kept for 3 years.

469
00:44:44.265 --> 00:44:56.959
Illustrative ₹/GB-month: hot (DB /. search cluster) 8.0. object standard 1.9, infrequent 1.0, archive 0.08.

470
00:44:57.239 --> 00:44:58.860
Compression ratio applied:

471
00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:00.484
6.3x.

472
00:45:00.764 --> 00:45:01.788
(measured above).

473
00:45:02.068 --> 00:45:03.583
The rest is in the chapter.

474
00:45:06.433 --> 00:45:07.286
Reading it:

475
00:45:07.566 --> 00:45:08.803
Encoding (measured):

476
00:45:09.083 --> 00:45:20.305
JSON 245 bytes or event; gzip 6.3x smaller; compact binary 6.3x; binary + gzip 9.0x.

477
00:45:20.585 --> 00:45:23.230
Real, and worth doing for high-volume streams.

478
00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:25.942
Proportionality decides everything else.

479
00:45:26.222 --> 00:45:33.091
The order events engineers debate cost 2 lakh rupees over three years even kept hot and raw, a rounding error.

480
00:45:33.371 --> 00:45:40.326
Application logs (~1,000x the volume) and clickstream (~200x) are where the money is:

481
00:45:40.606 --> 00:45:49.139
23.2 crore rupees hot and raw versus 29.3 lakh rupees compressed and tiered (illustrative prices).

482
00:45:49.419 --> 00:45:52.171
Retention policy is the lever, not encoding.

483
00:45:53.521 --> 00:46:01.735
Cross-zone transfer is small at this scale (~22 thousand rupees per month) and zone-aware routing cuts it ~13x.

484
00:46:02.015 --> 00:46:07.284
It becomes a major line only for data-heavy systems, check the size before investing.

485
00:46:07.564 --> 00:46:13.964
The FinOps rule the lab demonstrates: rank the streams by size first, then optimise the big ones.

486
00:46:15.314 --> 00:46:16.253
Part 6.

487
00:46:16.533 --> 00:46:17.813
Production Concerns.

488
00:46:19.063 --> 00:46:20.215
Failure modes.

489
00:46:20.495 --> 00:46:21.860
Here is a quick table:

490
00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:24.423
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

491
00:46:24.703 --> 00:46:25.748
Unowned spend.

492
00:46:26.028 --> 00:46:26.839
Symptom:

493
00:46:27.119 --> 00:46:30.447
Shared or untagged lines grow with no one accountable.

494
00:46:30.727 --> 00:46:31.729
Mitigation:

495
00:46:32.009 --> 00:46:35.977
Tag policy at provisioning; driver-based allocation (L1).

496
00:46:36.257 --> 00:46:37.729
Paying for peak all year.

497
00:46:38.009 --> 00:46:38.820
Symptom:

498
00:46:39.100 --> 00:46:41.212
Single-digit average utilisation.

499
00:46:41.492 --> 00:46:42.495
Mitigation:

500
00:46:42.775 --> 00:46:45.612
Autoscaling + calendar pre-scale (L2).

501
00:46:45.892 --> 00:46:47.684
Under-provisioning to save money.

502
00:46:47.964 --> 00:46:48.775
Symptom:

503
00:46:49.055 --> 00:46:52.020
Overload during sales, the most valuable hours.

504
00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:53.303
Mitigation:

505
00:46:53.583 --> 00:46:57.316
Pre-scale known events; target utilisation below the queueing knee.

506
00:46:57.596 --> 00:46:58.791
Committing to the peak.

507
00:46:59.071 --> 00:46:59.881
Symptom:

508
00:47:00.161 --> 00:47:02.572
Idle reserved capacity, already paid.

509
00:47:02.852 --> 00:47:03.855
Mitigation:

510
00:47:04.135 --> 00:47:07.441
Commit to the confident floor; ratchet quarterly (L3).

511
00:47:07.721 --> 00:47:09.449
Runaway logs or metrics.

512
00:47:09.729 --> 00:47:10.540
Symptom:

513
00:47:10.820 --> 00:47:13.487
Observability cost growing faster than traffic.

514
00:47:13.767 --> 00:47:14.769
Mitigation:

515
00:47:15.049 --> 00:47:18.975
Log levels, sampling, cardinality limits, retention (L4).

516
00:47:20.325 --> 00:47:21.455
Cut the wrong thing.

517
00:47:21.735 --> 00:47:22.546
Symptom:

518
00:47:22.826 --> 00:47:25.770
Removing an expensive service that drives revenue.

519
00:47:26.050 --> 00:47:27.053
Mitigation:

520
00:47:27.333 --> 00:47:30.746
Cost with value: experiments, unit margin (L1).

521
00:47:31.026 --> 00:47:32.242
Surprise invoice.

522
00:47:32.522 --> 00:47:33.333
Symptom:

523
00:47:33.613 --> 00:47:35.981
Misconfiguration found a month later.

524
00:47:36.261 --> 00:47:37.263
Mitigation:

525
00:47:37.543 --> 00:47:39.570
Daily anomaly detection per team.

526
00:47:39.850 --> 00:47:41.045
Non-prod sprawl.

527
00:47:41.325 --> 00:47:42.135
Symptom:

528
00:47:42.415 --> 00:47:44.378
Staging costs rival production.

529
00:47:44.658 --> 00:47:45.661
Mitigation:

530
00:47:45.941 --> 00:47:49.397
Off-hours shutdown, ephemeral environments, smaller shapes.

531
00:47:50.747 --> 00:47:52.219
Observability of cost.

532
00:47:52.499 --> 00:48:01.523
A cost panel on every service dashboard: daily cost, cost per request per order, trend, next to latency and errors (Ch 24).

533
00:48:01.803 --> 00:48:09.120
Anomaly alerts on per-team and per-service cost deviations, with the top driver (resource, SKU) in the alert.

534
00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:18.787
Utilisation dashboards: requested versus used CPU or memory per workload (the gap is waste), commitment utilisation and coverage.

535
00:48:19.067 --> 00:48:26.043
Observability is itself a cost: sample traces, cap metric cardinality, and set per-team log budgets.

536
00:48:27.393 --> 00:48:28.331
Security.

537
00:48:28.611 --> 00:48:36.121
Cost anomalies are also security signals: cryptomining on a compromised account shows up as a compute spike first.

538
00:48:36.401 --> 00:48:44.251
Enforce budgets and quotas per account or project so a leaked credential or a runaway loop can't create unbounded spend.

539
00:48:44.531 --> 00:48:53.641
Deleting unused resources needs care: confirm ownership and check for backups and compliance retention before removal (Ch 36).

540
00:48:54.991 --> 00:48:56.313
Performance trade-offs.

541
00:48:56.593 --> 00:49:05.020
Target utilisation trades cost against tail latency (Ch 31 L3); set it per service by latency sensitivity.

542
00:49:05.300 --> 00:49:12.319
Cheaper storage tiers are slower (archive retrieval takes hours), match tier to access pattern, not just price.

543
00:49:12.599 --> 00:49:18.209
Spot interruptions add retries and tail latency; keep them off user-facing critical paths.

544
00:49:18.489 --> 00:49:29.604
Compression costs CPU; for hot paths, measure CPU versus bytes saved (usually a good trade for storage and transfer, not always for latency-critical RPC).

545
00:49:30.954 --> 00:49:32.447
Cost of FinOps itself.

546
00:49:32.727 --> 00:49:37.719
Tagging, allocation pipelines and a small FinOps team cost money and attention.

547
00:49:37.999 --> 00:49:53.487
Keep effort proportionate (§2.10): at 0.76 rupeesof cloud per 650 rupeesorder, the priority is removing waste and bad incentives, not squeezing every rupee at the expense of delivery.

548
00:49:54.837 --> 00:49:55.776
Part 7.

549
00:49:56.056 --> 00:49:57.763
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

550
00:49:59.013 --> 00:49:59.930
Watch out:

551
00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:00.850
1.

552
00:50:01.130 --> 00:50:03.327
Treating the bill as finance's problem.

553
00:50:03.607 --> 00:50:06.423
Engineers make the spending decisions every day.

554
00:50:06.703 --> 00:50:11.503
Without visibility and ownership, cost grows by default (§1.1).

555
00:50:12.853 --> 00:50:13.771
Watch out:

556
00:50:14.051 --> 00:50:14.755
2.

557
00:50:15.035 --> 00:50:17.083
Reporting cost by cloud product.

558
00:50:17.363 --> 00:50:21.693
EC2 ₹X, RDS ₹Y answers nobody's question.

559
00:50:21.973 --> 00:50:25.856
Report by team, service and unit of business value (L1).

560
00:50:27.206 --> 00:50:28.123
Watch out:

561
00:50:28.403 --> 00:50:29.150
3.

562
00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:30.966
Even-split shared costs.

563
00:50:31.246 --> 00:50:35.918
Charges teams for others' usage and removes the incentive to reduce your own.

564
00:50:36.198 --> 00:50:38.097
Allocate by drivers (L1).

565
00:50:39.447 --> 00:50:40.364
Watch out:

566
00:50:40.644 --> 00:50:41.348
4.

567
00:50:41.628 --> 00:50:43.697
Cutting by size instead of value.

568
00:50:43.977 --> 00:50:46.751
The biggest line may be the best investment (L1:

569
00:50:47.031 --> 00:50:48.801
8.5x return).

570
00:50:49.081 --> 00:50:50.767
Pair cost with measured value.

571
00:50:52.117 --> 00:50:53.034
Watch out:

572
00:50:53.314 --> 00:50:54.082
5.

573
00:50:54.362 --> 00:50:56.751
Sizing the fleet for the festival, all year.

574
00:50:57.031 --> 00:51:00.871
4% of provisioned capacity actually needed (L2).

575
00:51:01.151 --> 00:51:02.709
Autoscale and pre-scale.

576
00:51:04.059 --> 00:51:04.976
Watch out:

577
00:51:05.256 --> 00:51:06.003
6.

578
00:51:06.283 --> 00:51:09.248
Relying on reactive autoscaling for known spikes.

579
00:51:09.528 --> 00:51:14.221
A step faster than the control loop overloads the first minutes of a sale (L2).

580
00:51:14.501 --> 00:51:16.059
Pre-scale from the calendar.

581
00:51:17.409 --> 00:51:18.326
Watch out:

582
00:51:18.606 --> 00:51:19.353
7.

583
00:51:19.633 --> 00:51:21.745
Committing to the peak (or p90).

584
00:51:22.025 --> 00:51:27.849
Idle, prepaid capacity; in L3 committing to the p90 was worse than no commitment.

585
00:51:28.129 --> 00:51:29.793
Commit to the confident floor.

586
00:51:31.143 --> 00:51:32.060
Watch out:

587
00:51:32.340 --> 00:51:33.023
8.

588
00:51:33.303 --> 00:51:35.116
Infinite retention by default.

589
00:51:35.396 --> 00:51:40.665
Keep everything hot, just in case turns logs into the fastest-growing line (L4:

590
00:51:40.945 --> 00:51:45.553
23.2 crore rupees versus 29.3 lakh rupees).

591
00:51:45.833 --> 00:51:47.455
Retention per data class.

592
00:51:48.805 --> 00:51:49.722
Watch out:

593
00:51:50.002 --> 00:51:50.749
9.

594
00:51:51.029 --> 00:51:52.629
Optimising the small stuff.

595
00:51:52.909 --> 00:51:58.178
Compressing order events that cost 2 lakh rupees over three years while logs cost crores.

596
00:51:58.458 --> 00:51:59.994
Rank first (L4).

597
00:52:01.344 --> 00:52:02.261
Watch out:

598
00:52:02.541 --> 00:52:03.245
10.

599
00:52:03.525 --> 00:52:05.723
Spot instances on the critical path.

600
00:52:06.003 --> 00:52:08.520
Interruptions become customer-facing errors.

601
00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:11.531
Spot is for interruptible, retry-safe work.

602
00:52:12.881 --> 00:52:13.798
Watch out:

603
00:52:14.078 --> 00:52:14.910
11.

604
00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:17.131
Finding problems on the invoice.

605
00:52:17.411 --> 00:52:18.969
A month is far too slow.

606
00:52:19.249 --> 00:52:21.275
Daily anomaly detection per team.

607
00:52:22.625 --> 00:52:23.543
Watch out:

608
00:52:23.823 --> 00:52:24.612
12.

609
00:52:24.892 --> 00:52:27.047
Cost cutting that erodes reliability.

610
00:52:27.327 --> 00:52:33.364
Removing headroom, replicas or DR to hit a number, until the outage costs more than the savings.

611
00:52:33.644 --> 00:52:35.692
Cost is one quality among several.

612
00:52:37.042 --> 00:52:37.917
Part 8.

613
00:52:38.197 --> 00:52:39.541
Interview Q and A.

614
00:52:40.791 --> 00:52:41.623
Beginner.

615
00:52:41.903 --> 00:52:42.927
Question 1.

616
00:52:43.207 --> 00:52:44.359
What is FinOps?

617
00:52:44.639 --> 00:52:46.004
Thirty-second answer:

618
00:52:46.284 --> 00:53:04.673
The practice of managing cloud cost as a shared responsibility between engineering, finance and product: make spend visible and owned (inform), reduce waste and buy capacity smartly (optimise), and keep it that way with budgets, anomaly alerts and cost in design reviews (operate).

619
00:53:04.953 --> 00:53:08.815
The goal is maximum business value per rupee, not the lowest bill.

620
00:53:10.165 --> 00:53:11.167
Question 2.

621
00:53:11.447 --> 00:53:13.965
What is unit economics and why does it matter?

622
00:53:14.245 --> 00:53:15.610
Thirty-second answer:

623
00:53:15.890 --> 00:53:22.781
Expressing cost per unit of business value, per order, per active customer, per tenant, instead of as a total.

624
00:53:23.061 --> 00:53:32.277
It shows whether growth is efficient: a bill that doubles with cost per order flat is healthy; cost per order rising means efficiency is getting worse.

625
00:53:32.557 --> 00:53:36.247
It also makes costs comparable to revenue and margin per unit.

626
00:53:37.597 --> 00:53:38.643
Question 3.

627
00:53:38.923 --> 00:53:42.101
On-demand versus reserved or committed versus spot?

628
00:53:42.381 --> 00:53:43.747
Thirty-second answer:

629
00:53:44.027 --> 00:53:47.675
On-demand: pay as you go, most flexible, most expensive.

630
00:53:47.955 --> 00:53:57.021
Commitments (reserved instances, savings plans): commit to capacity or spend for 1 to 3 years for a big discount, paid whether used or not.

631
00:53:57.301 --> 00:54:05.195
Spot or preemptible: deep discount on spare capacity that can be reclaimed at short notice, only for interruptible work.

632
00:54:06.545 --> 00:54:07.611
Question 4.

633
00:54:07.891 --> 00:54:10.963
Why do tags matter so much in cloud cost management?

634
00:54:11.243 --> 00:54:12.609
Thirty-second answer:

635
00:54:12.889 --> 00:54:19.161
Tags (owner, service, environment, cost centre) are how spend is attributed to teams and services.

636
00:54:19.441 --> 00:54:30.278
Untagged resources have no owner, so nobody reviews or turns them off, they're where forgotten load-test instances, orphaned volumes and unattached IPs accumulate.

637
00:54:30.558 --> 00:54:33.459
Enforce tags at provisioning, not after the fact.

638
00:54:34.809 --> 00:54:35.812
Intermediate.

639
00:54:36.092 --> 00:54:37.201
Question 5.

640
00:54:37.481 --> 00:54:41.876
How do you allocate shared costs like Kafka, observability and networking?

641
00:54:42.156 --> 00:54:43.521
Thirty-second answer:

642
00:54:43.801 --> 00:54:46.852
By usage drivers that reflect who causes the cost:

643
00:54:47.132 --> 00:55:02.705
Kafka by bytes produced (or partitions), observability by log or metric or trace volume, NAT and egress by gigabytes out, cluster overhead by resource requests or requests served, non-prod proportional to production footprint.

644
00:55:02.985 --> 00:55:08.276
An even split is simple but shifts cost to low-usage teams and removes incentives.

645
00:55:09.626 --> 00:55:10.735
Question 6.

646
00:55:11.015 --> 00:55:12.658
How do you rightsize a service?

647
00:55:12.938 --> 00:55:14.303
Thirty-second answer:

648
00:55:14.583 --> 00:55:16.695
Compare what's provisioned with what's used:

649
00:55:16.975 --> 00:55:25.871
CPU or memory requests versus actual usage, replica counts versus demand over time, instance shape versus workload profile.

650
00:55:26.151 --> 00:55:40.509
Set a target utilisation that respects latency (often 50 to 70% for latency-sensitive services), autoscale to it, pre-scale for known events, and fix shape mismatches (memory-heavy on compute-optimised).

651
00:55:40.789 --> 00:55:42.879
Re-check after every major release.

652
00:55:44.229 --> 00:55:45.317
Question 7.

653
00:55:45.597 --> 00:55:48.627
Why isn't reactive autoscaling enough for a flash sale?

654
00:55:48.907 --> 00:55:50.272
Thirty-second answer:

655
00:55:50.552 --> 00:55:59.981
Autoscalers react to observed metrics with a lag, metric window, decision, scheduling, image pull, JVM warm-up, often several minutes.

656
00:56:00.261 --> 00:56:06.277
A sale that multiplies traffic 5x in seconds overloads the fleet before new capacity arrives.

657
00:56:06.557 --> 00:56:13.789
Known events should be pre-scaled from a calendar; unknown spikes need warm pools, fast-starting images and load shedding.

658
00:56:15.139 --> 00:56:16.163
Question 8.

659
00:56:16.443 --> 00:56:19.793
How do you reduce observability cost without going blind?

660
00:56:20.073 --> 00:56:21.438
Thirty-second answer:

661
00:56:21.718 --> 00:56:45.462
Log at INFO in production with DEBUG switchable per service; sample traces (head or tail-based, keeping errors and slow requests); control metric cardinality (no user IDs as labels); set retention by value, hot for days, archive for compliance; convert high-volume logs to metrics where you only need counts; and give teams per-service log budgets with alerts.

662
00:56:46.812 --> 00:56:47.879
Question 9.

663
00:56:48.159 --> 00:56:51.167
How do storage tiers and lifecycle policies work?

664
00:56:51.447 --> 00:56:52.812
Thirty-second answer:

665
00:56:53.092 --> 00:57:08.772
Data gets less valuable to access quickly as it ages, so you move it through cheaper tiers: hot database or SSD, then object storage standard, then infrequent access, then archive, with lifecycle rules doing the moves automatically.

666
00:57:09.052 --> 00:57:13.788
Each colder tier is cheaper per GB but slower or more expensive to read.

667
00:57:14.068 --> 00:57:17.332
Retention policies delete data you no longer need.

668
00:57:18.682 --> 00:57:19.450
Senior.

669
00:57:19.730 --> 00:57:20.775
Question 10.

670
00:57:21.055 --> 00:57:23.573
How much capacity should you commit to for a year?

671
00:57:23.853 --> 00:57:25.218
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:57:25.498 --> 00:57:38.319
Commit to the floor, not the peak: a marginal committed unit pays off only if it would be busy in at least (commit price ÷ on-demand price) of the hours, 60% at a 40% discount.

673
00:57:38.599 --> 00:57:51.037
Against a forecast, sweep the commitment level and pick the minimum-cost point; under uncertainty, check expected cost and regret across plausible futures, and ratchet up quarterly as the floor rises.

674
00:57:52.387 --> 00:57:53.517
Question 11.

675
00:57:53.797 --> 00:57:56.656
A service is the most expensive line on the bill.

676
00:57:56.936 --> 00:57:57.917
Do you cut it?

677
00:57:58.197 --> 00:57:59.563
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:57:59.843 --> 00:58:01.208
Not without its value.

679
00:58:01.488 --> 00:58:10.917
Measure what it contributes, an A or B test, attributed revenue, cost avoided, or its role in reliability, and compare with its fully allocated cost.

680
00:58:11.197 --> 00:58:17.341
If value clearly exceeds cost, optimise it (cheaper model, caching, rightsizing) rather than cut it.

681
00:58:17.621 --> 00:58:19.243
If it doesn't, challenge it.

682
00:58:20.593 --> 00:58:21.681
Question 12.

683
00:58:21.961 --> 00:58:24.499
Which architecture decisions most affect cost?

684
00:58:24.779 --> 00:58:26.145
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:58:26.425 --> 00:58:41.230
Service granularity (each service has an HA and operational floor); statefulness and startup time (determine how well you can autoscale); synchronous versus asynchronous processing (async flattens peaks); data placement,

686
00:58:41.510 --> 00:58:56.379
retention and replication (storage and transfer); tenancy model (pooling amortises, silos multiply); cells (duplicate infrastructure); serverless versus containers (different cost curves); and code efficiency in hot paths.

687
00:58:57.729 --> 00:58:59.009
Question 13.

688
00:58:59.289 --> 00:59:01.935
How do you detect cost problems before the invoice?

689
00:59:02.215 --> 00:59:03.580
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:59:03.860 --> 00:59:25.044
Daily cost data (billing export) joined with tags and usage drivers, per team and per service; anomaly detection on day-over-day and forecast deviations with the top driver named; budgets with alerts at 50 or 80 or 100%; and per-service cost panels so engineers see changes right after deploys.

691
00:59:26.394 --> 00:59:27.589
Question 14.

692
00:59:27.869 --> 00:59:30.855
How do you control non-production environment costs?

693
00:59:31.135 --> 00:59:32.501
Thirty-second answer:

694
00:59:32.781 --> 00:59:50.679
Shut them down outside working hours, use smaller instance shapes and fewer replicas, create ephemeral per-PR environments that are destroyed automatically, share heavy dependencies where isolation isn't needed, use spot for CI, and give non-prod its own budget and anomaly alerts.

695
00:59:52.029 --> 00:59:52.925
Architect.

696
00:59:53.205 --> 00:59:54.400
Question 15.

697
00:59:54.680 --> 00:59:59.437
Design a FinOps operating model for a 200-engineer organisation.

698
00:59:59.717 --> 01:00:01.083
Thirty-second answer:

699
01:00:01.363 --> 01:00:08.147
Engineers own their services' cost; a small central FinOps function owns tooling, commitments and policy.

700
01:00:08.427 --> 01:00:27.008
Every resource tagged at provisioning (policy as code); shared costs allocated by drivers; cost per order and per service on every team dashboard; budgets and daily anomaly alerts per team; commitments purchased centrally against the confident floor and reviewed quarterly; retention and tiering

701
01:00:27.288 --> 01:00:34.947
defaults per data class; cost sections in design docs; and a monthly review of unit economics with product and finance.

702
01:00:36.297 --> 01:00:37.491
Question 16.

703
01:00:37.771 --> 01:00:41.291
How do cost and reliability trade off, and how do you decide?

704
01:00:41.571 --> 01:00:42.937
Thirty-second answer:

705
01:00:43.217 --> 01:00:52.283
Reliability costs money, headroom, replicas, multi-zone, DR regions, cells, and cutting it saves money until an outage costs more.

706
01:00:52.563 --> 01:01:07.262
Decide by pricing both sides: expected outage cost (probability times duration times cost per minute, Ch 34 L3) against the reliability spend, tiered by the business criticality of each service.

707
01:01:08.612 --> 01:01:09.956
Question 17.

708
01:01:10.236 --> 01:01:14.076
How would you reduce cost by 30% without harming the product?

709
01:01:14.356 --> 01:01:15.721
Thirty-second answer:

710
01:01:16.001 --> 01:01:37.228
Rank spend by owner and driver, then attack structural waste first: autoscale and pre-scale instead of static peak fleets; commit to the confident floor; enforce retention and tiering on logs and clickstream; shut down idle and non-prod capacity; remove orphans; fix requests-versus-usage gaps; move interruptible work to spot.

711
01:01:37.508 --> 01:01:44.655
Only then look at code efficiency and architecture changes, and pair every cut with its value and reliability impact.

712
01:01:46.005 --> 01:01:47.178
Question 18.

713
01:01:47.458 --> 01:01:50.786
How do you forecast cloud cost for a new product or region?

714
01:01:51.066 --> 01:01:52.431
Thirty-second answer:

715
01:01:52.711 --> 01:02:09.607
Build a driver-based model: business volume forecasts (orders, users, events), then resource drivers (requests, storage, transfer), then cost via unit prices and your current cost per unit, including fixed floors (HA, non-prod, observability).

716
01:02:09.887 --> 01:02:18.271
Express demand as ranges and run it as a Monte Carlo to get expected cost and a p90; revisit monthly against actuals.

717
01:02:19.621 --> 01:02:20.859
Question 19.

718
01:02:21.139 --> 01:02:24.616
How do you make engineers care about cost without slowing them down?

719
01:02:24.896 --> 01:02:26.261
Thirty-second answer:

720
01:02:26.541 --> 01:02:28.952
Visibility and good defaults, not gates.

721
01:02:29.232 --> 01:02:46.832
Show cost next to latency on their dashboards; make cost per order a team metric; put cheap-by-default choices in the paved road (autoscaling templates, finite retention, INFO logging, right-sized base images); alert them quickly on anomalies; and celebrate savings.

722
01:02:47.112 --> 01:02:50.973
Reserve reviews for large commitments and one-way-door decisions.

723
01:02:52.323 --> 01:02:53.433
Question 20.

724
01:02:53.713 --> 01:02:56.763
What distinguishes a principal engineer's view of cost?

725
01:02:57.043 --> 01:02:58.409
Thirty-second answer:

726
01:02:58.689 --> 01:03:09.185
They treat cost as a design quality with explicit trade-offs, express it in business units, connect it to value, and focus on structural levers rather than one-off cuts.

727
01:03:09.465 --> 01:03:23.886
They know which architecture choices set the cost curve, quantify decisions under uncertainty (commitments, serverless versus containers), design guardrails so savings persist, and keep effort proportionate to where the money actually goes.

728
01:03:25.236 --> 01:03:26.132
Part 9.

729
01:03:26.412 --> 01:03:27.820
Summary and Practice.

730
01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:30.158
Key takeaways.

731
01:03:30.438 --> 01:03:37.713
Cost is an architecture quality with owners, budgets and trade-offs, not a finance afterthought (§2.1).

732
01:03:37.993 --> 01:03:47.017
Allocate every rupee, direct, shared by usage drivers, untagged driven to zero, and express it per order, per customer, per tenant (L1).

733
01:03:47.297 --> 01:03:52.481
Judge spend by value, not size: the biggest line may be the best investment (L1).

734
01:03:52.761 --> 01:03:59.203
Autoscale the daily curve, pre-scale known events, keep target utilisation below the queueing knee (L2).

735
01:04:00.553 --> 01:04:08.447
Commit to the confident floor, never the peak; the curve is flat near the optimum, so avoid the tails and ratchet quarterly (L3).

736
01:04:08.727 --> 01:04:15.468
Retention and tiering beat encoding for big streams; rank data by size before optimising (L4).

737
01:04:15.748 --> 01:04:24.345
Design for cost: service granularity, startup time, async processing, tenancy and cells set the cost curve (§2.8).

738
01:04:24.625 --> 01:04:34.844
Run FinOps continuously: inform, optimise, operate, with anomaly alerts, policy as code and cost in design docs (§2.9).

739
01:04:36.194 --> 01:04:44.941
Be proportionate: remove waste and bad incentives; don't trade delivery speed or reliability for pennies (§2.10).

740
01:04:46.291 --> 01:04:47.613
Decision cheat sheet.

741
01:04:47.893 --> 01:04:49.259
Here is a quick table:

742
01:04:49.539 --> 01:04:50.861
Question, and Answer.

743
01:04:51.141 --> 01:04:52.827
How do we see where money goes?:

744
01:04:53.107 --> 01:04:57.480
Tags + driver-based allocation, then cost per service and per order.

745
01:04:57.760 --> 01:04:59.765
Is this expensive service worth it?:

746
01:05:00.045 --> 01:05:03.885
Compare allocated cost with measured value (A or B, margin).

747
01:05:04.165 --> 01:05:05.680
How big should the fleet be?:

748
01:05:05.960 --> 01:05:09.693
Autoscale to a target utilisation; pre-scale known events.

749
01:05:09.973 --> 01:05:11.253
How much to commit?:

750
01:05:11.533 --> 01:05:18.573
Up to where the marginal unit is busy ≥ commit or on-demand ratio of hours; ratchet.

751
01:05:18.853 --> 01:05:19.643
Spot?:

752
01:05:19.923 --> 01:05:22.248
Only interruptible, retry-safe work.

753
01:05:22.528 --> 01:05:23.957
Logs and clickstream?:

754
01:05:24.237 --> 01:05:27.736
Finite retention, compression, lifecycle tiers, sampling.

755
01:05:28.016 --> 01:05:29.275
Cross-zone costs?:

756
01:05:29.555 --> 01:05:32.541
Check size first; zone-aware routing where it matters.

757
01:05:32.821 --> 01:05:34.507
How do we avoid surprises?:

758
01:05:34.787 --> 01:05:37.368
Daily anomaly alerts and budgets per team.

759
01:05:38.718 --> 01:05:39.657
New design?:

760
01:05:39.937 --> 01:05:43.435
Cost section: monthly cost, cost per unit, biggest lever.

761
01:05:44.785 --> 01:05:45.916
Exercises.

762
01:05:46.196 --> 01:05:53.108
(B2) Add a cost-centre column to L1's bill and produce showback per team (a team owns several services).

763
01:05:53.388 --> 01:05:56.716
Which team's number changes most between allocation methods?

764
01:05:56.996 --> 01:06:03.097
(B2) In L2, change the target utilisation to 45% and 75%.

765
01:06:03.377 --> 01:06:05.767
How do cost and degraded minutes change?

766
01:06:06.047 --> 01:06:08.841
Where would you set it for checkout versus a batch service?

767
01:06:09.121 --> 01:06:13.644
(B3) Extend L3 with a 3-year commitment at 60% off.

768
01:06:13.924 --> 01:06:17.807
When does it beat the 1-year option under the same demand uncertainty?

769
01:06:19.157 --> 01:06:25.983
(B3) In L4, add Parquet-like columnar encoding (group each field across events before compressing).

770
01:06:26.263 --> 01:06:29.634
How much smaller than row-wise binary + gzip is it?

771
01:06:29.914 --> 01:06:38.618
(B4) Build a cost model for launching the platform in a new region: fixed floors, variable costs per order, cross-region replication.

772
01:06:38.898 --> 01:06:40.818
What's the break-even order volume?

